\description{
Fits a model of an outcome on the pivot ILR coordinates of a time-use
composition (plus optional covariates), with the model class selected by
\code{model}. The composition comes entirely from a \code{compD} object created by
\code{\link[=declare_composition]{declare_composition()}}. The coordinates enter linearly, unless
\code{curvature = TRUE} adds their full quadratic or \code{formula} writes other terms.
}
\section{Total as a covariate}{

The \eqn{\sqrt{D}} factor makes \code{t_total} the coordinate of \eqn{\log x}
along \eqn{\mathbf{1}/\sqrt{D}}, which completes the ILR basis to an
orthonormal one (Coenders et al. 2017, eq. 3.2). Any positive rescaling,
such as a \eqn{\sqrt{D-1}} convention, rescales its coefficient inversely
and leaves the fit, the predictions and \code{total_test} unchanged.
}

\section{Convergence}{

Every engine is fitted with its backend warnings and messages captured (and
still re-emitted). The fit carries two additive elements: \code{fit_warnings}, a
tibble of the captured conditions (\code{source}, \code{type}, \code{message}; zero rows
when clean) whose \code{source} is \code{"canonical"} or a rotated part name, and
\code{convergence}, a one-row tibble (\code{converged}, \code{singular}, \code{n_warnings}).
\link[=summary.comp_iv]{summary()} prints the captured messages and, for a
clustered or iterative fit, a \code{Convergence: OK} line when clean;
\link[=print.timecoda_fit]{print()} flags the warning count. \code{"cox"} and \code{"coxme"}
fits are not probed (\code{converged} is \code{NA}), so they print no such line. An
\code{"lmer"} or \code{"glmer"} fit that fails to converge or is singular can be
refitted with a different optimizer through \code{model_args} (e.g.,
\code{model_args = list(control = lme4::lmerControl(optimizer = "bobyqa"))}).
}

\section{Degrees of freedom for clustered fits}{

For an \code{"lmer"} fit, the per-part standard errors, intervals and p-values
that \link[=summary.comp_iv]{summary()} reports and the reallocations of
\code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}} use the same degrees-of-freedom method, chosen by the
rule \code{emmeans} applies. The default is the Kenward-Roger approximation
through \pkg{pbkrtest}. The Satterthwaite approximation through
\pkg{lmerTest} is used when \code{emmeans::emm_options(lmer.df = "satterthwaite")}
is set, or when \pkg{pbkrtest} is absent, disabled or the fit has more
observations than \code{emmeans::emm_options(pbkrtest.limit =)} (3000). Both
\pkg{pbkrtest} and \pkg{lmerTest} are optional, and when neither can be
used, or \code{lmer.df = "asymptotic"} is set, the degrees of freedom are
asymptotic (normal). \code{"glmer"}, \code{"cox"}, \code{"coxme"} and \code{"rlmer"} fits are
asymptotic in both places (\pkg{robustlmm} reports no degrees of freedom),
and \code{"lmrob"} fits use their residual degrees of freedom.
}

\examples{
cd <- declare_composition(timeuse, c("MVPA", "LPA", "SB", "SPT"),
                          closure = 1440)
m <- comp_iv(cd, outcome = "obesity", model = "glm", family = "binomial")
m$omnibus

# The full quadratic in the coordinates, a predicted outcome that can curve
curved <- comp_iv(cd, outcome = "zbmi", model = "lm", curvature = TRUE)
curved$omnibus

}
